'''Burrow Mump''' is a hill and historic site overlooking [[Southlake Moor]] in the village of [[Burrowbridge]] within the English county of [[Somerset]]. It is a [[scheduled monument]], with a never completed church on top of the hill a Grade II [[listed building]].

The hill stands at a strategic location overlooking the point where the [[River Tone]] and the old course of the [[River Cary]] join the [[River Parrett]]. Although there is some evidence of Roman visitation, the first fortification of the site was the construction of a [[Norman architecture|Norman]] [[Motte-and-bailey castle|motte]]. It has been called King Alfred's Fort, however there is no proof of use by [[Alfred the Great]]. A medieval church was built on the hill in the 15th century. The current ruined church on top of the hill was built in 1793. The land and ruin were donated to the [[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]] in 1946 as a war memorial.

==Geology==
[[File:Burrow Mump - geograph.org.uk - 1628142.jpg|thumb|Burrow Mump from the village of [[Burrowbridge]]]]
The hill is {{convert|24|m|ft}} high,<ref>{{cite web|title=Burrow Mump|url=http://www.imaginethegarden.co.uk/burrowmump|publisher=European Garden Heritage Network|access-date=5 October 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101217090411/http://www.imaginethegarden.co.uk/burrowmump|archive-date=17 December 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref> and stands at a strategic point where the [[River Tone]] and the old course of the [[River Cary]] join the [[River Parrett]], above the surrounding low-lying land of the [[Somerset Levels]].{{sfn|Hawkins|1982|p=16}} It is made of [[Triassic]] [[sandstone]] capped by [[Keuper marl]] ascribed to the [[Mercia Mudstone Group]].{{sfn|Hawkins|1982|p=16}}<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.bgs.ac.uk/discoveringGeology/geologyOfBritain/viewer.html |publisher=British Geological Survey |title=Geology of Britain viewer|access-date=22 January 2015}}</ref>

==Early use==

Burrow Mump is also known as St Michael's Borough or Tutteyate.<ref>{{cite web|title=Burrow Mump|url=http://www.gatehouse-gazetteer.info/English%20sites/3244.html|publisher=The Gatehouse|access-date=5 October 2010}}</ref> Both words 'burrow' and 'mump' mean hill.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.philipcoppens.com/glastonbury.html | title=Glastonbury: England's oldest sacred landscape? | author=Philip Coppens | author-link=Philip Coppens (author) |access-date=12 November 2010}}</ref> There is also an alternative translation for the name 'burrows' deriving from 'burh helow' meaning 'the refuge fort' and munp meaning 'a hill' so this becomes 'the refuge fort on the hill', this is very near Lyng which is where one of the Burghal Hideage forts recorded for King Alfred is located.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://saxonhistory.co.uk/FORMS-Page-Builder.php?Pg=914AD_Burghal_Hideage | title=Anglo Saxon Chronicles - AD914 - Alfred the Great and the Burghal Hideage | author=Simon | access-date=23 October 2024}}</ref>

Archaeological surveys have shown some [[Roman Britain|Roman]] material including a piece of pottery<ref name=tauntoncourier/> and coins found nearby which, possibly linked to its situation at a river junction, may indicate its use for trade.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Jenkins|first1=Palden|title=Burrowbridge Mump|url=http://www.palden.co.uk/leymap/burrowbridge-mump.html|publisher=Palden Jenkins|access-date=21 May 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Burrow Mump, Burrowbridge|url=http://www.somersetheritage.org.uk/record/44199|website=Somerset Historic Environment Record|publisher=Somerset County Council|access-date=21 May 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Roman coin hoard, Burrow Wall Rhyne, Burrowbridge|url=http://www.somersetheritage.org.uk/record/44200|website=Somerset Historic Environment Record|publisher=Somerset County Ouncil|access-date=21 May 2015}}</ref> Square pits, one of which may have been a well and post holes from the [[Middle Ages]] have been identified during excavations, these may have been from an [[adulterine castle]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Burrow Mump, Burrowbridge|url=http://www.gatehouse-gazetteer.info/English%20sites/3244.html|publisher=Gatehouse|access-date=21 May 2015}}</ref><ref name=nhle>{{NHLE|desc=Burrow Mump: a motte castle, later chapel and associated earthworks|num=1011823|access-date=21 May 2015}}</ref><ref name=pastscape/> It is likely that it was a [[Norman architecture|Norman]] [[Motte-and-bailey castle|motte]] with a terraced track that spirals around the hill to reach it.<ref name="sher">{{cite web|title=Burrow Mump, Burrowbridge|url=http://www.somersetheritage.org.uk/record/44199|work=Somerset Historic Environment Record|publisher=Somerset County Council|access-date=4 October 2010}}</ref> The plateau at the top is {{convert|45|m|sigfig=2}} by {{convert|25|m|sigfig=1}} and along with the scarped top of the slope formed the motte, which may have been formed during [[The Anarchy]] between 1135 and 1153.<ref name=nhle/> The site has been called [[Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] Fort, but there is no evidence of it being a fort<ref name=pastscape>{{PastScape|mname=Burrow Mump|mnumber=192181|access-date=3 March 2014}}</ref> or having any link with Alfred the Great,{{sfn|Adkins|1992|pp=32-33}} apart from its ownership by the nearby [[Athelney Abbey]] which he established and was linked to Burrow Mump by a causeway.<ref name=aerialsurvey>{{cite web|title=Report for Aerial Survey Component Block 2: Somerset Levels|url=http://archaeologydataservice.ac.uk/archiveDS/archiveDownload?t=arch-759-1/dissemination/pdf/phase_1/Interim_Report_Block_2.pdf|work=The Aggregate Landscape of Somerset: Predicting the Archaeological Resource|publisher=English Heritage|access-date=3 March 2014|pages=24–25}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Burrow Mump|url=https://wp.eghn.org/en/gardens/united-kingdom/burrow-mump|publisher=European Garden Heritage Network|access-date=21 May 2015}}</ref>{{sfn|Williams|Williams|1992|p=44}} It may have served as a natural [[outwork]] to the defended royal island of [[Athelney]] at the end of the 9th century.<ref>{{cite map |publisher=National Trust, Levels and Moors Partnership |title= Interpretive signs at the foot of Burrow Mump & brochure/map }}</ref>

Excavations have shown evidence of a 12th-century masonry building on the top of the hill, which may be from the probable adulterine castle.<ref name=tauntoncourier>{{cite news|title=The Burrow Mump Excavations|url=http://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000528/19390422/074/0003|access-date=22 January 2015|work=Taunton Courier, and Western Advertiser|date=22 April 1939| via = [[British Newspaper Archive]]|url-access=subscription }}</ref> The side of the mound may have been terraced for agricultural use due to much of the surrounding land flooded on a regular basis during the medieval period.<ref name=aerialsurvey/>

[[File:Burrow Mump.JPG|thumb|left|The ruins of St Michael's Church on top of Burrow Mump]]
The first recorded writing mentioning this site is from [[William Worcester]] in about 1480 when he referred to it as ''Myghell-borough''. A [[medieval]] church dedicated to St Michael, belonging to the Athelney Abbey,{{sfn|Hawkins|1982|pp=121-122}} dates from at least the mid-15th century. This formed a sanctuary for [[Cavalier|royalist troops]] in 1642 and 1645 during the [[English Civil War]],{{sfn|Quinn|2008|pp=22-23}} and a detachment of the king's army occupied it in 1685 during the course of the [[Monmouth Rebellion]].{{sfn|Adkins|1992|pp=32-33}}

==18th century rebuilding==
In 1793, the church was rebuilt with a west tower, 3-bay nave and south porch, in squared and coursed [[Lias Group|lias]] with red brick and [[Hamstone]] dressings.<ref name=nhle/> The attempt at total rebuilding ended in failure to collect enough money, despite donations from [[William Pitt the Younger]] and [[Sir Samuel Hood, 1st Baronet|Admiral Hood]],<ref name="sher"/> and a church for the community was built instead at the foot of the hill ([[Burrowbridge]]) in 1838.<ref name=nhle/> In the mid 20th century the ruin on Burrow Mump underwent some repairs to the north west corner.<ref name=nhle/>

The ruined church is one of the churches dedicated to St. Michael that falls on a [[ley line]] proposed by [[John Michell (writer)|John Michell]] of the pseudoscientific Earth Mysteries Movement. Other connected St. Michaels on the ley line include churches built at [[Othery]] and [[Glastonbury Tor]].<ref>{{cite web|title=The St. Michael's Ley, England|url=http://www.ancient-wisdom.co.uk/stmichael.htm#stmichaelsley|publisher=Ancient-Wisdom|access-date=5 October 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100922123706/http://ancient-wisdom.co.uk/stmichael.htm#stmichaelsley|archive-date=22 September 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref> However, Michell's ley line is drawn as a straight line on a map across the counties of Wiltshire, Somerset, Devon and Cornwall linking his sites, and does not take into account the curvature of the Earth. Churches built on hills were also frequently dedicated to St Michael, and it is just a coincidence that a small scattering of churches dedicated to St Michael are found roughly near to the route of his line.

==Memorial==
[[File:Plaque on St Michaels Chapel on Burrow Mump (geograph 2834284).jpg|thumb|Memorial plaque]]
The site of {{convert|3.573|ha}} including the hill and ruined church were presented, in 1946, by Major [[Alexander Barrett|Alexander Gould Barrett]],<ref>{{cite web |title=Acquisitions Up to December 2011 |url=http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/cs/Satellite?blobcol=urldata&blobheader=application%2Fpdf&blobheadername1=Content-Disposition&blobheadername2=MDT-Type&blobheadername3=Content-Type&blobheadervalue1=inline%3B+filename%3D297%252F965%252Fnt_acquisitions_dec2011-2%252C2.pdf&blobheadervalue2=abinary%3B+charset%3DUTF-8&blobheadervalue3=application%2Fpdf&blobkey=id&blobtable=MungoBlobs&blobwhere=1349117284549&ssbinary=true |publisher=National Trust |page=6 |access-date=22 January 2015 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714144751/http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/cs/Satellite?blobcol=urldata&blobheader=application%2Fpdf&blobheadername1=Content-Disposition&blobheadername2=MDT-Type&blobheadername3=Content-Type&blobheadervalue1=inline%3B+filename%3D297%2F965%2Fnt_acquisitions_dec2011-2%2C2.pdf&blobheadervalue2=abinary%3B+charset%3DUTF-8&blobheadervalue3=application%2Fpdf&blobkey=id&blobtable=MungoBlobs&blobwhere=1349117284549&ssbinary=true |archive-date=14 July 2014 }}</ref> to the [[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]] and serve as a memorial to the 11,281{{sfn|Dunning|1983|p=101}} Somerset men who lost their lives during the [[World War I|First]] and [[World War II|Second World Wars]].{{sfn|Leete-Hodge|1985|p=82}} The National Trust was taken to court for [[Nuisance in English law|nuisance]] after soil from Burrow Mump slipped onto a neighbouring farmer's land.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Fea|first1=Vivennne|title=Means Test Liability: The Subjective Standard of Care in Nuisance|journal=Otago Law Review|date=1981|volume=417|url=http://heinonline.org/HOL/LandingPage?handle=hein.journals/otago5&div=29&id=&page=}}</ref> The ruin was classified as a Grade II [[listed building]] in 1963.<ref>{{NHLE |num=1344609 |desc=Remains of Church on Burrow Mump|access-date=5 April 2015}}</ref> It has been a [[Scheduled monument]] since 1949.<ref name=nhle/>

